WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:04.930
This video is made in partnership with Nikon
Healthcare, a leader in scientific imaging

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:06.220
and tools.

00:00:06.220 --> 00:00:10.500
You can learn more about their imaging technology
and microscopy solutions by visiting the link

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:24.619
in the description.

00:00:24.619 --> 00:00:28.720
In labs around the world, you can find tanks
like this one.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:35.600
They are filled with zebrafish, who have received
that name for obvious stripe-related reasons.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.699
These particular zebrafish are residents of
the National Institutes of Health, where they

00:00:39.699 --> 00:00:45.699
provide researchers with the means to study
disease and understand more about how life develops.

00:00:45.699 --> 00:00:51.420
And here, thanks to the power of fluorescent
proteins and microscopy, we see the zebrafish

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:57.570
in a whole new palette, lit up in electric
shades of blue and orange.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:04.140
This image is first and foremost discovery,
the result of a scientific hunt for a series

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:11.159
of vessels inside the zebrafish whose existence
might help scientists understand human diseases.

00:01:11.159 --> 00:01:13.390
But we’ll get to that in a bit.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:18.750
Because what we can also appreciate is that
this image is stunning.

00:01:18.750 --> 00:01:25.080
There’s the clarity and contrast of the
colors, the sense of texture as the scales  begin to overlap.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:30.360
You don’t have to understand everything
going on in this image to find it beautiful…just

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:32.409
like any art, really.

00:01:32.409 --> 00:01:36.570
Which is why it’s perhaps not surprising
that in 2020, the scientist who took this

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:49.110
picture submitted it to the Nikon Small World
Photomicrography Competition and won.

00:01:49.110 --> 00:01:55.549
The Nikon Small World Competition began in
1975 with the photomicrography contest, providing

00:01:55.549 --> 00:02:01.700
a showcase for all of the spectacular imagery
that many masters of microscopes have observed

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:05.859
and created, using whatever subject or technique
that suits them.

00:02:05.859 --> 00:02:12.080
And in 2011, Nikon added a new video category
called Small World in Motion.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:17.910
Every year, Nikon gathers a panel of judges
to go through the submissions and decide on the winner.

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:26.130
And in 2021, that panel of judges included
me, your trusty Journey to the Microcosmos host Hank Green.

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:31.390
I was there, as you might imagine, not so
much for my technical expertise as I was to

00:02:31.390 --> 00:02:35.980
get excited about and interested in the stories
behind the images.

00:02:35.980 --> 00:02:41.600
And I said yes, not just for the free camera
that I did get, but also to have a couple

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:47.830
of days to sit around with true experts in
their fields and to get some real behind the

00:02:47.830 --> 00:02:52.860
scenes insights into science that you can’t
really get by reading articles, and I was

00:02:52.860 --> 00:03:02.010
not disappointed by the insights of my fellow
judges, who are true experts and were really lovely people.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:08.730
The 2021 winner of the Small World in Motion
competition was this spectacular clip of symbiotic

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:16.380
protists that live inside of a termite’s
gut, taken and submitted by Fabian J. Weston from Australia.

00:03:16.380 --> 00:03:21.890
Filmed with polarized light and a microscope
from the 1970s, you can see how much these

00:03:21.890 --> 00:03:26.480
organisms resemble organisms that you have
seen in our episodes.

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.790
But these organisms have managed to carve
out a home for themselves inside of termites,

00:03:31.790 --> 00:03:36.110
where they help their host digest wood and
other plants.

00:03:36.110 --> 00:03:42.560
This is one of the transportive experiences
of microscopy, the way that one image can

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:48.480
reveal a world that is both so alien and unifying
at once.

00:03:48.480 --> 00:03:53.200
And if there’s an animal that has embodied
that dichotomy over and over again, it is

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:58.350
the one we started this episode with: the
zebrafish.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:06.890
Now, on the one hand, it doesn’t seem like
we should be finding a lot in common with

00:04:06.890 --> 00:04:08.590
a striped minnow.

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:13.710
This is a fish that spawns eggs when daylight
breaks; that has those eggs fertilized outside

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:17.969
of its body; and that will produce hundreds
of offspring through this method over the

00:04:17.969 --> 00:04:20.169
course of a week.

00:04:20.169 --> 00:04:24.789
And their embryos develop really quickly,
assembling a head, tail, and heart within

00:04:24.789 --> 00:04:27.129
24 hours of fertilization.

00:04:27.129 --> 00:04:32.860
So when you hear all that—the daylight spawning,
the external fertilization, the rapid development—you

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:37.569
probably would not expect to find much common
ground with the zebrafish.

00:04:37.569 --> 00:04:46.169
Except that we do share common ground—we
share about 71% of our genome with a zebrafish,

00:04:46.169 --> 00:04:51.259
and many of those genes are valuable counterparts
to understanding human diseases.

00:04:51.259 --> 00:04:59.460
This combination of similarities and differences
make zebrafish an extraordinary resource for scientists.

00:04:59.460 --> 00:05:03.749
As an embryo and larva, the zebrafish is transparent.

00:05:03.749 --> 00:05:08.969
And with the right combination of pigment
mutations, scientists have been able to create

00:05:08.969 --> 00:05:12.449
a ghostly, translucent version of the fish
called casper.

00:05:12.449 --> 00:05:18.030
That clarity makes it much easier for scientists
to observe the inner workings of the fish

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:24.259
under the microscope compared to, you know,
much more opaque animals, like us or mice.

00:05:24.259 --> 00:05:29.449
And since zebrafish are easy to take care
of and regularly make embryos, they provide

00:05:29.449 --> 00:05:36.340
many, many opportunities for researchers to
search for equivalents to our own selves in the fish.

00:05:36.340 --> 00:05:40.380
In the case of the image we just showed at
the beginning of the episode, what researchers

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:46.060
were pursuing was a series of lymphatic vessels
inside the zebrafish’s skull.

00:05:46.060 --> 00:05:51.370
In mammals, these vessels maintain the right
balance of fluids in our nervous system, making

00:05:51.370 --> 00:05:55.740
them potentially important in understanding
diseases like Alzheimers.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:06:01.370
And being able to use zebrafish to study these
vessels would be amazing…except for one

00:06:01.370 --> 00:06:07.490
problem: no one had ever observed lymphatic
vessels in the skull of non-mammals.

00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:12.699
No one, that is, until Daniel Castranova and
his fellow scientists in Brant Weinstein’s

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:14.160
lab at the NIH.

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:19.430
They used fluorescent proteins in transgenic
zebrafish to highlight the lymphatic vessels,

00:06:19.430 --> 00:06:21.580
bones, and scales.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:27.520
And you can see those parts of the zebrafish
here: the lymphatic vessels are shown in orange,

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:30.639
the bones and scales are shown in blue.

00:06:30.639 --> 00:06:37.189
To take these images, the researchers anesthetized
a juvenile zebrafish and mounted it on agarose

00:06:37.189 --> 00:06:38.870
to hold it still.

00:06:38.870 --> 00:06:43.039
Then they used a confocal microscope to divide
and image the fish in layers.

00:06:43.039 --> 00:06:49.150
It’s a bit like cutting the fish into cross-sections,
only there’s no need to make a physical cut.

00:06:49.150 --> 00:06:53.900
The microscope creates those cross-sections
through its own optical methods.

00:06:53.900 --> 00:07:01.340
The result is around 387 images that could
then be stitched together to create one final image.

00:07:01.340 --> 00:07:04.300
But this image is not just a 2-D photograph.

00:07:04.300 --> 00:07:11.650
It is a three-dimensional reconstruction,
creating a map of cells inside the zebrafish

00:07:11.650 --> 00:07:16.379
that tells the researchers which vessels are
inside and outside the skull.

00:07:16.379 --> 00:07:21.479
This map revealed that zebrafish have lymphatic
vessels inside their skulls.

00:07:21.479 --> 00:07:25.759
Moreover, researchers can easily do live imaging
of these vessels.

00:07:25.759 --> 00:07:31.259
They can even see immune cells traveling through
those vessels.

00:07:31.259 --> 00:07:36.180
What the final product reveals is not just
something new, and not just something beautiful.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:42.620
What this image reveals really is the possibility
we continue to find in zebrafish.

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:47.849
Much of this possibility is scientific, a
future where experiments with zebrafish contribute

00:07:47.849 --> 00:07:51.330
to a greater understanding of disease.

00:07:51.330 --> 00:07:56.589
There is so much work that goes into making
images like this one and all the other Nikon

00:07:56.589 --> 00:08:03.360
Small World submissions, so much careful thought
and technique to determine how to use biology

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:05.599
and technology at once.

00:08:05.599 --> 00:08:11.300
And all of it hinges on the microscope, a
scientific and artistic instrument rolled

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:17.889
into one that has allowed its many masters
to craft visions of worlds that contain bits

00:08:17.889 --> 00:08:22.800
of ourselves.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:28.240
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:32.330
And thank you again to Nikon Healthcare for
helping us make this video.

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:37.450
If you want to learn more about the imaging
technology that helps to create amazing microscopy

00:08:37.450 --> 00:08:41.160
like what you saw in this video, make sure
to check out the link in the description,

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:45.960
and if you’d like to see more imagery and
video that blends science and art, check out

00:08:45.960 --> 00:08:49.050
the microscopy galleries at NikonSmallWorld.com.

00:08:49.050 --> 00:08:53.420
I know I have spent a lot of time there.

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:57.140
A big thanks to all of the people whose names
are on the screen right now.

00:08:57.140 --> 00:08:59.720
They are our patrons on Patreon.

00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:05.290
Patreon is a place where people support the
things that they think should exist in the world.

00:09:05.290 --> 00:09:09.350
And all of these people have decided that
they want Journey to the Microcosmos to exist

00:09:09.350 --> 00:09:12.830
in the world, and gosh darn it, if we don’t
agree with them.

00:09:12.830 --> 00:09:17.710
So, thank you all so much, we obviously could
not do this without your support.

00:09:17.710 --> 00:09:23.120
If you would like to become one of these people,
you can do that at patreon.com/journeytomicro.

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:26.520
If you want to see more from our Master of
